  "grid_title": "WAYS TO HURT YOUR ANKLE",
  "why": "Rolled ankle (lateral sprain): most ankle sprains (around 90%) are inversion injuries (the foot turns in) to the outside ligaments, the anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) most often. High ankle sprain: the ligaments joining the tibia and fibula just above the ankle (the syndesmosis), usually from the foot twisting outward; less common, slower to heal. Broken ankle: isolated fractures of the end of the fibula (the lateral malleolus, the outer bump) are the majority of ankle fractures. Fifth metatarsal: when the foot and ankle twist down and in, a ligament can pull a fragment off the base of the bone on the outside of the foot (an avulsion fracture). Peroneal tendons: two tendons that run behind the outer ankle bone; they can be inflamed (tendonitis), torn, or slip out of place (subluxation). Achilles rupture: the largest tendon in the body; a pop, and a feeling like being kicked in the lower leg, often in sports with jumps and quick starts. Trouble putting weight on it: get it checked.",
